--- /dev/null
+++ b/android/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,124 @@
+# ST Reborn for Android
+
+The STR desktop app, running on an Android phone or tablet: the same Go core,
+the same frontend, the same features apart from the few that need a computer.
+
+STR is an unofficial, community-built project. It is not affiliated with,
+endorsed by, or authorized by Bose. "Bose" and "SoundTouch" are trademarks of
+Bose Corporation.
+
+## How it works
+
+Wails, which hosts the desktop app, has no Android target. But the desktop
+app talks to Wails in only two places: on the Go side through `wails.Run` and
+the `runtime` package (events, file dialogs, window calls), and on the
+frontend side through the generated `window.go.main.App.*` bindings and
+`window.runtime`. The port replaces Wails at exactly those two seams and
+nothing else.
+
+```
+ ┌──────────────── Android app (android/app) ─────────────────┐
+ │ WebView  https://wails.localhost/  (unchanged frontend)    │
+ │   android-shim.js: window.go.main.App  +  window.runtime   │
+ │        │ STRHost (JavascriptInterface)        ▲ events      │
+ │ HostBridge.java ── pickers, dialogs, URLs, clipboard        │
+ │        │ JNI (UTF-8 byte arrays)              │             │
+ │ libstr.so = desktop-app built with -buildmode=c-shared      │
+ │   mobile_android.go / mobile_jni.c   (entry points)         │
+ │   android/wailsshim  (stands in for github.com/wailsapp/... │
+ │                       wails/v2 via go.android.mod)          │
+ └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
+```
+
+* **Go side.** `desktop-app/go.android.mod` is `go.mod` plus one `replace`
+  that points `github.com/wailsapp/wails/v2` at `android/wailsshim`. The
+  stand-in mirrors the Wails v2.16 API signature for signature, so the
+  desktop app compiles unmodified. Behind it, `pkg/mobile` dispatches bound
+  calls by reflection (the same argument and error conventions as Wails),
+  queues events for the WebView, and turns dialogs into requests the Android
+  host answers.
+* **Frontend side.** The build copies the unchanged frontend into the APK and
+  loads `android-shim.js` ahead of it. The shim recreates `window.go` (as a
+  Proxy, so new bindings need no change) and `window.runtime`.
+  `android.css` adds a few phone-specific corrections, all scoped to
+  `html.platform-android`.
+* **Origin.** The page is served from `https://wails.localhost`, which is the
+  origin Wails uses on Windows. Every speaker agent already allows it in
+  `allowedCORSOrigins`, so the frontend's direct speaker requests work against
+  agents in the field, with no agent change and no loosening of that list.
+
+The only change to an existing file is `desktop-app/crashlog_unix.go`, which
+used `syscall.Dup2`. That call does not exist on arm64 Linux, so the desktop
+app did not compile for an ARM Linux desktop either. It now uses
+`unix.Dup2` from `golang.org/x/sys`.
+
+## Android-specific behavior
+
+| Topic | What the port does |
+|---|---|
+| Speaker discovery | Holds a Wi-Fi `MulticastLock` so mDNS and SSDP packets reach the app. |
+| Network interfaces | Android 11+ forbids the netlink calls behind `net.Interfaces()` (golang/go#40569). The build patches the standard library's `net` package with `go build -overlay` (`goport/overlay`), adding a bind-free `RTM_GETADDR` + `ioctl` fallback. Every caller, including zeroconf, works unchanged. |
+| DNS | Android has no `/etc/resolv.conf`, so resolvers pinned to the pure-Go path (the update client's) would query `127.0.0.1`. The host writes the current network's DNS servers to a private `resolv.conf` and the overlay points Go at it. |
+| Speaker setup AP | While Wi-Fi is connected the process is bound to it. A new speaker's own access point has no internet, so Android would otherwise send the core's traffic over mobile data. |
+| Save / open file | Wails "save to path" becomes: the method writes to a private staging file, and when the call returns the system "Save as" picker copies it to wherever the user chooses. "Open" uses the system picker and hands Go a private copy. |
+| Closing mid-update | Back asks the core first (the desktop `OnBeforeClose`), which offers to stay while an install or update runs. The screen is kept on while one runs. |
+| App self-update | Hidden: the desktop updater has no Android build to fetch. Speaker agent updates are unaffected. |
+| USB stick | Hidden: a phone app cannot see or format USB drives. The network install, the default route, is unchanged. |
+
+## Building
+
+Requirements: Go (the version in `go.mod`), Node and npm, and the Android SDK
+and NDK. Android Studio installs the SDK and NDK (SDK Manager > SDK Tools >
+NDK).
+
+```sh
+android/build.sh                   # native library, assets, then the APK
+WITH_ENGINE=1 android/build.sh     # also embed the go-librespot engine
+SKIP_GRADLE=1 android/build.sh     # stop before Gradle (then open android/ in Android Studio)
+ABIS=arm64-v8a android/build.sh    # a single ABI, for faster iteration
+```
+
+Like `make wails-build`, the script stamps the app and its embedded speaker
+agent with the same `git describe` version, so the OTA comparison stays
+consistent. Nothing tracked is modified: the agent and engine reach their
+`go:embed` slots through the same overlay, and the frontend is built outside
+`frontend/dist`.
+
+Release signing: set `STR_KEYSTORE`, `STR_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD`, `STR_KEY_ALIAS`
+and `STR_KEY_PASSWORD`. Without them the release APK is signed with the debug
+key. That is fine for testing but not for distribution.
+
+## Trying the Android build in a browser
+
+On a desktop OS the stand-in's `wails.Run` serves the frontend with the
+Android shim and carries the bridge over HTTP:
+
+```sh
+cd desktop-app/frontend && npm run build && cd ..
+go run -modfile=go.android.mod .   # then open http://127.0.0.1:34115/
+```
+
+This runs the exact Go code the phone runs and is useful with devtools in
+device mode. Port 34115 is the `wails dev` port, which agents already allow,
+so it works against real speakers too.
+
+## Tests
+
+* `cd android/wailsshim && go test -race ./...` covers the dispatcher
+  (Wails return and error conventions, argument decoding, panics), the event
+  and host-request queues, and the export handover.
+* The JNI layer and the net overlay were checked on a Linux host by building
+  the same sources under a test tag and driving them from a plain JVM and a
+  small Go program. See the PR description.
+
+## Open items
+
+* Verified on real hardware: not yet. The bridge, overlay and UI were
+  exercised against the real core, but no APK has run on a phone against a
+  speaker.
+* Newer Android versions are adding a runtime permission for local-network
+  access. `targetSdk` stays at 35 until that is handled.
+* App updates: none yet (sideloaded APK). A Play Store or F-Droid listing,
+  or an in-app APK update, is a separate decision.
+* The application ID `de.streborn.android` follows the desktop's
+  `de.st-reborn.desktop` naming and is the maintainer's to change.
